<setup> 
  <selectFromSequence name="a" from="2" to="4" step="2"/>
  <selectFromSequence name="k" from="3" to="7" step="2"/>

  <function name="f">ln(1+$k*x^$a)</function>
  
</setup>

<p>
  Our goal in this exercise is to find the general antiderivative of the function 
  <me>
    f(x) = \ln(1+$k x^$a)
  </me>
</p>

<p>
  <ol marker="a.">
    <li>
      <p>
        Use a familiar Taylor series expansion along with algebraic techniques to find the first four nonzero terms of the Taylor series centered at <m>0</m> for <m>f(x)</m>.  State those four respective terms in the provided blanks below.
      </p> 
      <p>
        <m>f(x) = </m><answer>$k*x^($a)</answer><m>+</m><answer>-1/2*$k^2*x^(2*$a)</answer><m>+</m><answer>1/3*$k^3*x^(3*$a)</answer><m>+</m><answer>-1/4*$k^4*x^(4*$a)</answer><m>+ \cdots</m>
      </p> 
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        Determine the open interval of convergence for the Taylor series of <m>f(x) = \ln(1+$k x^$a)</m>.  If the series converges for all real numbers, enter <m>(-INF, INF)</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        The Taylor series of <m>f(x)</m> converges on <answer allowedErrorInNumbers="0.001" allowedErrorIsAbsolute>(-1/$k^(1/$a),1/$k^(1/$a))</answer>.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        Use the Power Series Differentiation and Integration Theorem to find the first <em>five</em> nonzero terms of the Taylor series for <m>F(x)</m>, the general antiderivative of <m>f(x)</m>.  Enter those terms in order of increasing degree in the blanks provided; if you need to include a constant of integration, use <q><m>C</m></q>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>F(x) = </m><answer>C</answer><m>+</m><answer>$k/($a+1)*x^($a+1)</answer><m>+</m><answer>-$k^2/(2*(2*$a+1))*x^(2*$a+1)</answer><m>+</m><answer>$k^3/(3*(3*$a+1))*x^(3*$a+1)</answer><m>+</m><answer>-$k^4/(4*(4*$a+1))*x^(4*$a+1)</answer><m>+ \cdots</m>
      </p> 
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        Determine the open interval of convergence for the Taylor series of <m>F(x)</m>.  If the series converges for all real numbers, enter <m>(-INF, INF)</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        The Taylor series of <m>F(x)</m> converges on <answer allowedErrorInNumbers="0.001" allowedErrorIsAbsolute>(-1/$k^(1/$a),1/$k^(1/$a))</answer>.
      </p>
    </li>    
  </ol>
</p>